+1.2 The doublet derivative is $D_\mu H=\partial_\mu H+igW_\mu H+\tfrac{i}{2}g'B_\mu H$, because $Y(H)=1/2$. Applying the product rule to $H=(v+h)Ue_2/\sqrt2$ and using $T_3e_2=-e_2/2$ in the $D_\mu U$ formula of [F2] yields
 $$D_\mu H=\frac1{\sqrt2}\left((\partial_\mu h)Ue_2+(v+h)D_\mu Ue_2\right).$$
 The conjugate formula follows by complex conjugation. Repeated covariant derivatives expand into finite sums by the product rule, and field strengths remain the same curvature polynomials under this substitution. Thus every selected local polynomial representative has a finite HEFT expansion. [given, F1, F2, F5, algebra]
 
-1.4 Each undifferentiated $H$ or $\widetilde H$ in a representative contributes one factor $v+h$; each differentiated doublet contributes, by step 1.3, a sum whose terms contain at most one such factor. The source representatives contain at most six scalar fields by [F3]. Therefore every product term after the finite Leibniz expansion has radial degree at most six. Writing $x=h/v$ only extracts fixed powers of $v$ into its coefficient, so every radial coefficient is a polynomial in $x$ of degree at most six and hence analytic. The finite selected representatives produce finitely many HEFT monomials; define $R_{\alpha k,a}$ to be the coefficient of the $k$th radial power in monomial slot $\alpha$ after the stated substitution and dimensional normalization. Linearity of substitution then gives $f_{\alpha k}=\sum_aR_{\alpha k,a}c_a$, which is the claimed finite linear-image constraint. [given, F3, step 1.3, algebra]
